{
  "about": "Sleevesight genre vocabulary: the tags that become genre labels, by family, with the family hue (design/design.md, Genre labels). First taken from GENRE_FAMILIES in design/simulator.html. labels overrides the default label, the tag in title case. synonyms groups spellings of one genre beyond punctuation and spacing, which give one label. musicbrainz gives each tag the MusicBrainz genre (CC0) through which its definition is found: the genre's Wikidata item and its English Wikipedia article give the definition a genre's panel shows (#523). A tag whose name, folded to its letters and digits, is one of MusicBrainz's genres has that genre (matched against MusicBrainz's genre list on 2026-10-27, and for the tags added in #1011 on 2026-11-06); a tag that is not one, or whose genre has no English article, has the nearest genre that has one, where one fits (#663, checked 2026-10-27): alternative and indie are alternative rock and indie rock, as Last.fm's listeners use them; goth and gothic are gothic rock; metal is heavy metal; punk is punk rock; chanson francaise is chanson française; rap is hip hop; neoclassical is neoclassical new age, the contemporary piano and strings Last.fm's listeners tag so; modern classical is contemporary classical, and contemporary country is country (#1011, checked 2026-11-06). wikipedia gives a tag its English Wikipedia article by title, for a tag with no MusicBrainz genre that fits or whose genre has no English article, where an article says what the tag means (#852, checked 2026-11-02): acoustic is Acoustic music, score Film score, choral Choir, orchestral Orchestra, world music World music (#1011, checked 2026-11-06); a tag with one is defined by it, not through MusicBrainz. #1011 (2026-11-06) widened the list with most of the genres MusicBrainz's tags use most that it dropped (the genre cloud of MusicBrainz's 200 most used genre tags, read on 2026-11-06; not those that are no style of music, such as non-music, comedy, ballad or concerto, nor the loosest, progressive and club), the genres of the cases seen on the phone (psychedelic rock, neo-psychedelia, Anatolian rock, which turkish psychedelia is a synonym of, afrobeat, world music), and a few of the common genres of other regions; and a family, world, for music named by its region rather than by a style of the other families (Latin, Brazilian, African, Iberian, Turkish folk music). The tags left without either are listed, each with why, in GenreDefinitionsTest.",
